  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Cloud Spanner in a multi region topology together with Google Bare Metal Solution to mirror the on premises environment</b>. This pairing delivers strongly consistent transactions with synchronous replication and a near zero RPO for the database, and it provides a like for like bare metal landing zone in Google Cloud so failover can occur quickly and order processing can resume.</p><p>In a multi region configuration, <b>Cloud Spanner</b> replicates writes synchronously across regions using quorum based consensus which provides strict consistency and an RPO of zero for regional or zonal failures. It also performs automatic leader election and failover, so recovery is fast and operationally simple.</p><p><b>Google Bare Metal Solution</b> offers dedicated physical servers in Google data centers with low latency connectivity, which allows you to mirror the on premises stack while maintaining performance characteristics. With appropriate network and storage replication this environment can be promoted quickly during a site outage, which satisfies the fast failover requirement for latency sensitive services.</p><p><i>Cloud Spanner with a multi region configuration and Google Anthos Bare Metal for replicating the on premises estate</i> is not suitable because Anthos bare metal is a Kubernetes platform for running containerized workloads on your own hardware and it does not provide a managed disaster recovery target in Google Cloud that mirrors a non containerized bare metal estate.</p><p><i>AlloyDB for PostgreSQL with cross region read replicas and Cloud VMware Engine to mirror the bare metal apps</i> does not meet the goals because AlloyDB cross region replicas use asynchronous replication which results in an RPO greater than zero and requires promotion. The VMware service mirrors VMware based workloads rather than general bare metal services and would likely require re platforming.</p><p><i>Cloud Bigtable with multi cluster routing and Google Bare Metal Solution for the on premises footprint</i> cannot satisfy the consistency requirement because Bigtable does not provide transactional semantics and multi cluster replication is eventually consistent. This combination cannot guarantee strongly consistent writes or an RPO that is very close to zero.</p>",
